th display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Φ</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Φ</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{Φ} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> induces an emf.</strong> This process is defined to be <span data-type="term" id="import-auto-id1169737778764" group-by="e">electromagnetic induction</span>. Units of magnetic flux <em data-effect="italics"><span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Φ</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Φ</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{Φ} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span></em> are <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⋅</mo><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⋅</mo><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{T cdot m rSup { size 8{2} } } {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span>. As seen in <a href="23-1-induced-emf-and-magnetic-flux#import-auto-id1169737961842" class="autogenerated-content" aria-label="View Figure 23.6">Figure 23.6</a>, <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mspace width="0.25em"></mspace><mtext>cos</mtext><mspace width="0.25em"></mspace><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mspace width="0.25em"></mspace><mtext>cos</mtext><mspace width="0.25em"></mspace><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{B"cos"θ=B rSub { size 8{ ortho } } } {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span><sub>⊥</sub>, which is the component of <em data-effect="italics"><span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{B} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span></em> perpendicular to the area <em data-effect="italics"><span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{A} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span></em>. Thus magnetic flux is <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mtext>⊥</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mtext>⊥</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{Φ=B rSub { size 8{ ortho } } A} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span>, the product of the area and the component of the magnetic field perpendicular to it.</p><div class="os-figure" id="import-auto-id1169737961842"><figure data-id="import-auto-id1169737961842" class="ui-has-child-figcaption"><span data-type="media" id="import-auto-id1169737804415" data-alt="Figure shows a flat square shaped surface A. The magnetic field B is shown to act on the surface at an angle theta with the normal to the surface A. The cosine component of magnetic field B cos theta is shown to act parallel to the normal to the surface.">
            <button type="button" aria-label="Click to enlarge image of Figure shows a flat square shaped surface A. The magnetic field B is shown to act on the surface at an angle theta with the normal to the surface A. The cosine component of magnetic field B cos theta is shown to act parallel to the normal to the surface." class="image-button-wrapper"><img src="/apps/image-cdn/v1/f=webp/apps/archive/20260604.144757/resources/19fc1947d3a665f85fad98fdf457ede6e49b7418" data-media-type="image/jpg" alt="Figure shows a flat square shaped surface A. The magnetic field B is shown to act on the surface at an angle theta with the normal to the surface A. The cosine component of magnetic field B cos theta is shown to act parallel to the normal to the surface." width="750" height="759" loading="lazy" data-original-src="/apps/archive/20260604.144757/resources/19fc1947d3a665f85fad98fdf457ede6e49b7418"></button>
        </span>
            
        <figcaption class="os-caption-container">
  <span class="os-title-label">Figure </span>
<span class="os-number">23.6</span>



  <span class="os-divider"> </span>
  <span class="os-caption">Magnetic flux <math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Φ</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Φ</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{Φ} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math> is related to the magnetic field and the area over which it exists. The flux <math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><mstyle fontstyle="italic"><mrow><mtext>BA</mtext></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mspace width="0.25em"></mspace><mtext>cos</mtext><mspace width="0.25em"></mspace><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><mstyle fontstyle="italic"><mrow><mtext>BA</mtext></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mspace width="0.25em"></mspace><mtext>cos</mtext><mspace width="0.25em"></mspace><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{Φ= ital "BA""cos"θ} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math> is related to induction; any change in <math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Φ</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Φ</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{Φ} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math> induces an emf.</span>
</figcaption></figure></div><p id="import-auto-id1169738074843">All induction, including the examples given so far, arises from some change in magnetic flux <em data-effect="italics"><span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Φ</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Φ</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{Φ} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span></em>. For example, Faraday changed <em data-effect="italics"><span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{B} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span></em> and hence <em data-effect="italics"><span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Φ</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation